# ============================================================================
# ignore-scripts=true — the single highest-leverage supply-chain control here.
# ============================================================================
#
# THE THREAT. `npm ci` installs ~600 packages. By default npm executes the
# `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` hook of EVERY ONE of them, as the invoking
# user, before a single line of our code runs. That is the delivery mechanism
# behind essentially every real-world npm compromise (event-stream, ua-parser-js,
# node-ipc, the 2025 "Shai-Hulud" self-propagating worm): the attacker does not
# need you to *import* the package, only to *install* it. On CI that code runs
# next to GITHUB_TOKEN and the AZURE_* signing secrets; on a dev machine it runs
# next to the developer's SSH keys, npm token and gh credentials. With this flag
# a malicious version of a transitive dependency has to wait to be imported and
# executed, which for a build-time-only package may be never.
#
# WHY IT'S SAFE HERE. Re-audited against the committed lockfile after the
# electron 43 / electron-builder 26 / vite 7 bumps: 163 packages declare a
# lifecycle script of any kind, and all but FOUR declare only
# `prepare` / `prepack` / `prepublish*` / `postpublish`, none of which npm runs
# for a package installed from a registry tarball. The real install-time hooks
# are these four, and none of them is required:
#
#   esbuild            postinstall: node install.js  -> NOT required. The actual
#   tsx > esbuild      postinstall: node install.js     binary ships in the
#                                                      platform-specific optional
#                                                      dep (@esbuild/win32-x64),
#                                                      which npm installs as a
#                                                      normal package; install.js
#                                                      only re-validates it.
#                                                      Verified: esbuild
#                                                      transforms fine without it.
#   onnxruntime-node   postinstall: node script/...  -> NOT required on Windows.
#                                                      Its only job is fetching the
#                                                      Linux/x64 CUDA execution
#                                                      providers; it exits 0
#                                                      immediately on win32. Every
#                                                      CPU binary we ship is inside
#                                                      the npm tarball (see the
#                                                      npmRebuild note in
#                                                      electron-builder.yml).
#   electron-winstaller install: select-7z-arch.js   -> NOT required. It arrived
#                                                      with electron-builder 26 via
#                                                      electron-builder-squirrel-
#                                                      windows, and it only picks a
#                                                      7z binary for the SQUIRREL
#                                                      target. We build `nsis` and
#                                                      only `nsis`, so nothing ever
#                                                      calls it.
#
# ELECTRON IS NO LONGER ON THAT LIST, and that is a real change, not an omission.
# Through 33.x, `electron` declared `postinstall: node install.js` and this flag
# suppressed it. As of the 43 line electron's published package.json declares NO
# scripts at all; instead `require('electron')` LAZILY spawns install.js when
# node_modules/electron/dist is missing. Two consequences worth being honest
# about:
#
#   * ignore-scripts no longer suppresses anything for electron. There is nothing
#     left to suppress.
#   * the ~190 MB download can now be triggered implicitly, by the first thing
#     that imports electron — which includes `npm test`, since several suites
#     import modules that reach `app`. It is the same @electron/get fetch over
#     HTTPS against the version pinned in package.json; it is just no longer
#     bounded to an explicit step.
#
# There are NO node-gyp native modules in the tree (onnxruntime-node ships
# N-API-3 prebuilds, no binding.gyp anywhere), so `electron-builder
# install-app-deps` / `node-gyp rebuild` are not part of this project's install.
#
# THE ONE RULE THIS CREATES: after `npm ci` / `npm install`, run
#
#     npm run deps:electron
#
# Since electron 43 this is a PRE-WARM rather than a hard requirement — the lazy
# path would fetch the same binary later — but keep doing it. It puts the
# download in a step you can see fail, with a log line and an exit code, instead
# of inside an unrelated test run. Both CI jobs do this explicitly
# (.github/workflows/build.yml). It is idempotent and a no-op once the binary
# matches.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# audit-level=moderate: `npm install` prints a summary rather than staying quiet
# about a moderate finding. It does not fail anything (that would be
# `audit-signatures` / `npm audit --audit-level`), it just refuses to be silent.
ignore-scripts=true
audit-level=moderate
